---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import GapSlider from '../islands/GapSlider';
import {
  DISGUISE_HOLD_PERCENT,
  DISGUISE_SECONDS,
  FEATURED,
  SITE,
  comparisonsFor,
  library,
  playHref,
} from '../lib/data';
import { NO_RESEMBLANCE_YARDS } from '../../src/engine/disguise';
import { FRAME_RATE_HZ } from '../../src/engine/frames';
import { capitalize, count, list, tidy } from '../lib/format';

const lib = library();
const { counts } = lib;
const featured = lib.conceptById.get(FEATURED.conceptId)!;
const featuredScheme = lib.schemeById.get(FEATURED.schemeId)!;
const worked = comparisonsFor(featured).map((c) => {
  const far = c.gapDetails;
  const detail =
    far.length === 0
      ? 'Everyone within 1 yd'
      : far.length <= 4
        ? `${list(far.map((g) => `${g.id} at ${g.yards.toFixed(1)} yd`))}. Everyone else within 1 yd`
        : `${capitalize(count(far.length))} players`;
  return { ...c, detail };
});
const frames = Math.round(DISGUISE_SECONDS * FRAME_RATE_HZ) + 1;
const contents = [
  ['A', 'illustrative', 'What illustrative means'],
  ['B', 'drawing', 'How a play is drawn'],
  ['C', 'similarity', 'How the similarity score works'],
  ['D', 'compiler', 'The optional spec compiler'],
  ['E', 'licence', 'Data and licence'],
  ['F', 'corrections', 'Corrections'],
];
---

<Base
  title="About and method"
  description={`What SchemeDB's ${counts.plays} plays are, how they are drawn, how two plays are scored against each other, and what you may do with the data.`}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="crumbs">
      <a href="/">Cover</a>
      <span class="sep"><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Appendix</span></span>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top:8px;padding-bottom:40px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
    <p class="eyebrow">Appendix · How this binder was made</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">About and method</h1>
    <p class="body" style="font-size:18px;line-height:28px">What the plays are, how they are drawn, how two plays are scored against each other, and what you may do with the data.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap appendix" style="padding-bottom:64px">
    <nav aria-label="Appendix contents">
      <div class="divider sm"><p class="eyebrow">Contents</p></div>
      <ol class="apx">
        {contents.map(([n, id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}><span class="n">{n}</span> <span>{label}</span></a></li>)}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="text">
      <section id="illustrative">
        <div class="divider"><p class="eyebrow">Appendix A</p><h2>What illustrative means</h2></div>
        <p class="body">All {counts.plays} plays are drawn by hand to show how a scheme works. They do not record a snap, a team or a game, and none of them comes from tracking data. The data marks each one <span class="type" style="color:var(--fg)">illustrative</span>, with no sources yet.</p>
        <p class="body">Read a play as a coach’s chalk drawing of the idea, not as film of a game. When plays gain sources, the record will say so.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="drawing">
        <div class="divider"><p class="eyebrow">Appendix B</p><h2>How a play is drawn</h2></div>
        <p class="body">Each play is a set of timed waypoints for 22 players. A waypoint is a position in yards, measured from the line of scrimmage and from the left sideline, at a moment after the snap. The viewer plays the waypoints back at {FRAME_RATE_HZ} frames a second and moves each player along a smooth curve through them.</p>
        <p class="body">On every field, offense attacks up the page, filled amber marks are offense, blue rings are defense, and the heavy line is the line of scrimmage.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="similarity">
        <div class="divider"><p class="eyebrow">Appendix C</p><h2>How the similarity score works</h2></div>
        <p class="body">At each tenth of a second from the snap to {DISGUISE_SECONDS.toFixed(1)} s, SchemeDB measures how far apart each player stands in the two plays and averages those gaps. A mean gap of 0 yards scores 100. A mean gap of {NO_RESEMBLANCE_YARDS} yards or more scores 0. Between them the score falls in a straight line. A play’s score is its average over the {frames} frames.</p>
        <p class="body">Players are matched by their label, so a player who appears in only one of the two plays is left out of that score. The play page says when that happens.</p>
        <p class="body">The {DISGUISE_HOLD_PERCENT}% line is SchemeDB’s own cutoff for plays that still look alike. It has not been tested against real defenders, so it compares plays and does not measure what a defense sees.</p>

        <div class="sheet try">
          <div class="strip"><p>The straight line rule</p><p>Try a mean gap</p></div>
          <div class="inner">
            <GapSlider client:visible />
            <div style="overflow-x:auto">
              <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Mean gap</th><th>Score</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  {[0, 1.5, 3, 4.5, NO_RESEMBLANCE_YARDS].map((gap) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td class="k type">{gap === NO_RESEMBLANCE_YARDS ? `${gap} yd or more` : `${gap} yd`}</td>
                      <td class="type">{Math.round(100 * (1 - gap / NO_RESEMBLANCE_YARDS))}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="slab" style="font-size:22px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:600;padding-top:8px">A worked example from {featuredScheme.nameDisplay}</h3>
        <p class="body">Each of the {count(worked.length)} related plays scored against {tidy(featured.name)}, over the first {DISGUISE_SECONDS.toFixed(1)} seconds.</p>
        <div class="only-wide" style="max-width:820px">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Play</th><th>Average, 0 to {DISGUISE_SECONDS.toFixed(1)} s</th><th>At {DISGUISE_SECONDS.toFixed(1)} s</th><th>More than 1 yd apart at {DISGUISE_SECONDS.toFixed(1)} s</th><th>The {DISGUISE_HOLD_PERCENT}% line</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {worked.map((c) => (
                <tr>
                  <td class="k">{c.name}</td>
                  <td class="type">{c.score}%</td>
                  <td class="type">{c.atEnd}%</td>
                  <td>{c.detail}</td>
                  <td>{c.breakSeconds === null ? 'Stays above it' : `Drops below it at ${c.breakSeconds.toFixed(1)} s`}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="only-compact">
          <div class="stack">
            {worked.map((c) => (
              <div class="row">
                <p style="color:var(--fg);font-weight:500;font-size:15px">{c.name}</p>
                <p><span class="type">{c.score}%</span> on average, <span class="type">{c.atEnd}%</span> at {DISGUISE_SECONDS.toFixed(1)} s. {c.breakSeconds === null ? `Stays above the ${DISGUISE_HOLD_PERCENT}% line.` : `Drops below the ${DISGUISE_HOLD_PERCENT}% line at ${c.breakSeconds.toFixed(1)} s.`}</p>
                <p>More than 1 yd apart at {DISGUISE_SECONDS.toFixed(1)} s: {c.detail}.</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="body">Scrub these numbers yourself on the <a href={`${playHref(featuredScheme.id, featured.id)}#compare`}>play sheet comparison</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="compiler">
        <div class="divider"><p class="eyebrow">Appendix D</p><h2>The optional spec compiler</h2></div>
        <p class="body">The spec compiler is a separate tool that turns a coach style play description into waypoints. Its timing constants are rounded medians from NFL Big Data Bowl 2025 player tracking (NFL Next Gen Stats, weeks 1 to 9 of the 2022 season, released by the NFL under CC BY NC 4.0). Source: <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/nfl-big-data-bowl-2025">NFL Big Data Bowl 2025 on Kaggle</a>.</p>
        <p class="body">Today the compiler has produced three sample plays and none of the {counts.plays} on the site. The {counts.plays} plays were drawn by hand, do not use its timing constants, and do not use tracking data.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="licence">
        <div class="divider"><p class="eyebrow">Appendix E</p><h2>Data and licence</h2></div>
        <p class="body">The data is {SITE.dataLicense}, so you may use it for anything if you credit SchemeDB. The code is {SITE.codeLicense}. Downloads come as JSON, as CSV tables and as a SQLite file, from the <a href="/data/">Data page</a>.</p>
        <p class="lab">Suggested credit</p>
        <p><span class="cred">{SITE.credit}</span></p>
        <p class="body">SchemeDB is not affiliated with the NFL, any college or any team named on the site.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="corrections">
        <div class="divider"><p class="eyebrow">Appendix F</p><h2>Corrections</h2></div>
        <p class="body">Found a mistake? Open an issue at <a href={SITE.repo}>github.com/harlanljones/scheme-db</a>. SchemeDB is an independent project by Harlan Jones.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
